{"type":"api-request-blob","sessionId":"473348c5-5f94-4e4d-9ad9-c7e9dd10b438","hash":"a7d29f311d7273aa3ca6d2e2f42310ea59050e3010ce1ebe54a890a053a957b7","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01JbE1qL5pg51xZgbJGGJqbq","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/Users/kenneth/Projects/JusPrin/design-captures/value-prop-2026-11/tagline/ai-a-ai-slicer.png"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01KsyP5B9CUMT23uqx3YQ2Qf","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/Users/kenneth/Projects/JusPrin/design-captures/value-prop-2026-11/animatic/beats/stringing-beat-4-slices-again.png"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_013TTRweityXuXs626atXW4S","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cd /Users/kenneth/Projects/JusPrin/design-captures/value-prop-2026-11/animatic/src; wc -l welcome-stringing.html animatic.html strings.js; cat strings.js | head -60; grep -o 'src=\"[^\"]*\"' welcome-stringing.html animatic.html | sort -u | head; sips -g pixelWidth -g pixelHeight benchy-model.png benchy-sliced.png | grep pixel","description":"Read the clip's strings and the image sources the reference renders use"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}]}}
{"type":"api-request-blob","sessionId":"473348c5-5f94-4e4d-9ad9-c7e9dd10b438","hash":"3c5c96d51372e6a40963dda95a15739ca2d0914b9304cefb018b331153581cfe","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01JbE1qL5pg51xZgbJGGJqbq","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"image","source":{"type":"base64","data":"","media_type":"image/jpeg"}}]},{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01KsyP5B9CUMT23uqx3YQ2Qf","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"image","source":{"type":"base64","data":"","media_type":"image/png"}}]},{"tool_use_id":"toolu_013TTRweityXuXs626atXW4S","type":"tool_result","content":"      39 welcome-stringing.html\n     136 animatic.html\n      58 strings.js\n     233 total\n// Every string the clip shows.\n// CHAT: verbatim from the vase run (journey-cost-2026-10-runs/out/j3-opus-photo, messages 15, 19, 32, 36); check-words.py asserts it.\n//   With the Benchy photos these are placeholders: a live run on the chosen photo will replace them.\n// PHOTOS.*.big: textbook fixes for each photo, not recorded agent output. STAGED: not app strings. APP: verbatim app strings (slicing: src/slic3r/GUI/JusPrin/Shell/LeftPane.cpp, _L(\"Slicing %d%%\")).\nwindow.STR = {\n \"CHAT\": {\n  \"user1\": \"I printed it and it failed.\",\n  \"user2\": \"What went wrong?\",\n  \"user3\": \"Photo attached.\",\n  \"reply19\": \"I'm sorry the print failed. Your photo shows the base rings went down, but once one side came loose the nozzle kept printing into thin air, which made the tangle of loose strands. I looked at the slice and the settings are normal for PLA on a textured PEI plate: bed at 55 °C, a 5 mm brim, and a slow first layer. So the most likely causes are these:\\n\\n1. The plate surface. The most common cause by far is grease from fingertips on the textured plate, which stops PLA from sticking. Wash the plate with warm water and dish soap, rinse it, let it dry, and then touch it only by the edges.\\n2. The plate on the bed. Check that the textured PEI plate is the one on the printer and that it sits flat. I set the job up for that plate, but I can't see which plate is actually there.\\n3. The vase's shape. The base is narrow, about 50 mm across, and the wall flares out to 85 mm. In spiral vase mode the wall is a single thin line, printed at up to 200 mm/s from about 1.5 mm up. If the edge of the base is only lightly stuck, that pulling and the cooling fan can lift one side, which matches what you saw at 5 mm.\\n\\nWhat I suggest: clean the plate first, since that alone often fixes it. I can also make the next try sturdier, if you'd like:\\n- a wider brim, 10 mm, for more grip\\n- a warmer bed, 60–65 °C\\n- slower speeds for the first few millimetres of wall\\n\\nI haven't changed anything yet. Should I apply these and slice again?\",\n  \"reply32\": \"I made all four changes and I'm slicing the vase again now:\\n\\n- Bed temperature: 55 → 60 °C\\n- Brim: 5 → 10 mm, around the outside of the base\\n- Cooling fan: off for the first 3 layers, then building up to full power by layer 8, instead of full power from layer 2\\n- Speed: the first 5 mm (25 layers) now print slowly and speed up gradually, instead of jumping to full speed early on\\n\\nThese changes apply to this project only. I haven't saved them into your PLA or print profiles, so other projects stay as they were.\\n\\nYou can watch the slice in the card below, and I'll check the results when it finishes. Before you print, close the window or block the draft. That matters more than any of these settings.\",\n  \"time\": \"1 h 34 min\",\n  \"filament\": \"34 g\"\n },\n \"PHOTOS\": {\n  \"thick\": {\n   \"file\": \"photo-thick.jpg\",\n   \"pos\": \"62% 50%\",\n   \"big\": [\n    [\n     \"Layer height\",\n     \"\"\n    ],\n    [\n     \"0.4 → \",\n     \"0.2 mm\"\n    ]\n   ]\n  },\n  \"stringing\": {\n   \"file\": \"photo-stringing.jpg\",\n   \"clean\": \"photo-clean.jpg\",\n   \"pos\": \"50% 45%\",\n   \"big\": [\n    [\n     \"Retraction 0.8 → \",\n     \"2 mm\"\n    ],\n    [\n     \"Nozzle 220 → \",\n     \"200 °C\"\n    ]\n   ]\n  }\n },\n \"APP\": {\n  \"slicing\": \"Slicing %d%%\"\n },\n \"STAGED\": {\n  \"ready\": \"Sliced · ready\"\n },\n \"CAPTIONS\": [\n  \"Show it what happened.\",\n  \"It reads your photo, your file and your slice.\",\n  \"It changes the settings for you.\",\n  \"And slices again. Tonight.\"\n ]\n};\nanimatic.html:src=\"benchy-model.png\"\nanimatic.html:src=\"benchy-sliced.png\"\nanimatic.html:src=\"strings.js\"\nwelcome-stringing.html:src=\"animatic.html?photo=stringing&t=2.2\"\n  pixelWidth: 539\n  pixelHeight: 478\n  pixelWidth: 539\n  pixelHeight: 478","is_error":false},{"type":"text","text":"[Image: original 2560x1600, displayed at 2000x1250. Multiply coordinates by 1.28 to map to original image.]"}]}}
